= Hawthorne effect =

== Overview ==

{{:Hawthorne effect/Simple}}

The '''Hawthorne effect''' names a loosely defined class of reported behavior changes associated with awareness of study participation, observation or special attention. Popular retellings of the original Hawthorne plant studies exaggerate evidence for a unitary mechanism; empirical research shows heterogeneous participation and measurement effects, with historic attribution and causal scope disputed.

== Three claims that must be separated ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Claim !! Evidence needed !! Status caveat
|-
| A change occurred during Hawthorne workplace studies || Archival measures and study chronology || Observational changes alone cannot establish causes
|-
| Researcher attention caused the change || Controlled or credible counterfactual contrast with other conditions held comparable || Classic story is contested by historical reanalysis
|-
| Knowing one is observed changes behavior in some studies || Modern randomized/natural experiments with defined reactivity measure || Effects vary in direction, magnitude and intervention mechanisms
|}

Reanalyses of archival illumination records have challenged the canonical productivity narrative. Systematic reviews identify evidence for some effects of research participation while emphasizing inconsistent definitions and methodological limitations.<ref name="historical_core">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720811417968 “Shining New Light on the Hawthorne Illumination Experiments”], ''Human Factors'' (2011), archival-data reanalysis and inconsistent illumination evidence.</ref><ref name="review_core">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3969247/ “Systematic Review of the Hawthorne Effect: New Concepts Are Needed to Study Research Participation Effects”], ''Journal of Clinical Epidemiology'' 67, 267–277 (2014).</ref>

== Mechanisms and method boundaries ==

Potential mechanisms include changes in motivation, novelty, perceived monitoring, demand characteristics, feedback and alterations in the work environment. They are different variables; treating all as “Hawthorne” can conceal the actual study design.

The subject connects to [[Related to::Scientific model|model identification]], [[Related to::Reproducibility|reproducibility]], [[Related to::Cognition|behavioral interpretation]] and [[Related to::Uncertainty|uncertainty]]. The canonical status “Disputed” concerns the historical and unified explanatory claim, not a denial that measurement and participation can sometimes influence behavior.

